          "source_structure_hash": "sha256:8e32468439f1c95afbcf171cde7dc7cfbe5971c62f0727288cf2978691ddde07",
          "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nAn algebraic stack is a stack in groupoids over the category of schemes\nwith the fppf topology that has a diagonal representable by algebraic\nspaces and is the target of a surjective smooth morphism from a scheme.\nSee Algebraic Stacks, Section <REF_0001>.\nA ``Deligne-Mumford stack'' is an algebraic stack for which there exists\na scheme and a surjective <ACCENT_0001>tale morphism from that scheme to it\nas in the paper <REF_0002> of Deligne and Mumford, see\nAlgebraic Stacks, Definition <REF_0003>.\nWe will reserve the term ``Artin stack'' for a stack such as in the papers by\nArtin, see <REF_0004>, <REF_0005>, and <REF_0006>.\nA possible definition is that an Artin stack is an algebraic stack\n<MATH_0001> over a locally Noetherian scheme <MATH_0002> such that\n<MATH_0003> is\nlocally of finite type<FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001>Namely, these are exactly the algebraic\nstacks over <MATH_0004> satisfying Artin's axioms [-1], [0], [1], [2], [3], [4], [5]\nof Artin's Axioms, Section <REF_0007>.<FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001>.\n\n\n",
          "source_text_hash": "sha256:69f38bf35079b2d5b6070b7a9cb3cb7c0ba70074943af24d5316812a0f3d0964",
          "unit_id": "tag:02BK:prose-0001"
        }
      ],
